nodecode-model-presets is a rewrite of will-lynas's will-lynas/hermes-model-presets:
https://github.com/will-lynas/hermes-model-presets
commit 27eec57bc17d92d931a025ed333b330dd17636e5
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 will-lynas (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's one idea, kept whole. It read a table of aliases out of its
own settings and turned a short `/model <alias>` into a full `/model <model> --provider
<provider> --reasoning <rung>` line at the harness gateway, failing open on anything it did
not recognise. This cell keeps the same table, the same words for it (a preset's model, its
provider, its reasoning effort, with "aliases" and "reasoning_effort" read too), and the same
overrides and fail-open posture; what it changes is where the pick lands. Nodecode already
owns the meaning of a model pick (SELECT-MODEL and SELECT-EFFORT: resolve the lane, check
the rung against that model's ladder, pin the session, move the default, tell every shell),
so the cell calls those two instead of rewriting a line someone has to retype, and puts the
table behind one slash command, /preset, and one model tool, model_presets.

What was left out: the Hermes gateway hook itself (pre_gateway_dispatch), which has no
Nodecode counterpart and nothing to do without a gateway command line to rewrite; the
`--global`, `--session` and `--refresh` flags, of which a Nodecode pick already does what
--global and --session meant, so they are accepted and ignored, and there is no provider
catalogue to refresh. Nothing else of the original was code: its parser, its validation and
its typed protocols came to one table read and one call.
